schema: concept-lineage
object:
  title: "Outputs vs. Outcomes vs. Impact"
  type: concept-lineage
  short_description: >-
    The core distinction the impact-measurement field rests on: outputs are
    countable activity ("we ran 12 workshops"), outcomes are what actually
    changed for people or ecosystems as a result ("30 farmers adopted a
    regenerative practice"), and impact is the difference the work made
    versus what would have happened anyway (if the trees would have been
    planted regardless, impact is zero even with 400 outputs).
  source_traditions:
    - impact evaluation / M&E (monitoring & evaluation)
    - theory of change methodology
    - MRV (measurement, reporting, verification) for ecological projects
  toolkit_usage: >-
    Central diagnostic frame for local-node builders in the article: most
    nodes count outputs because they're "easy and seductive," but funders
    and the node's own future self care about outcomes and impact; the
    article uses it to argue for lightweight before/after surveys and
    follow-ups rather than a research budget.
  adjacent_meanings:
    - "vanity metrics (activity counted for its own sake, named as the failure mode this distinction guards against)"
    - "theory of change (the one-page map from activities to desired change, used to decide which two or three numbers are worth collecting)"
  important_distinctions:
    - >-
      counting-activity-vs-demonstrating-change — "we ran 12 workshops"
      (output) tells no one anything on its own; "12 workshops, after which
      30 farmers switched to cover-cropping, up from 4 the year before"
      (outcome) is a story with a spine.
    - >-
      outcome-vs-counterfactual-impact — an outcome can occur without the
      node's work being the cause of it; impact specifically nets out what
      would have happened anyway.
  tensions:
    - >-
      rigor-vs-burnout — the article explicitly warns against "becoming a
      spreadsheet bureaucracy"; the recommended discipline is a small number
      of weekly-checked metrics, not comprehensive measurement.
  risks_of_flattening: >-
    Treating any counted number as equally meaningful "measurement" erases
    the distinction the whole article is built on — a high output count can
    coexist with zero real impact, and the article frames that gap as where
    "good local initiatives quietly die."
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/why-measurement-matters.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted

